In this lesson we'll look at the quadratic formula and how it lets you solve any quadratic equation, even when it doesn't factorise neatly. Any quadratic can be written as a times x squared plus b times x plus c equals zero, where a, b, and c are just numbers. The quadratic formula gives you the two possible values of x using those three numbers, and the trickiest bit is the part under the square root, called the discriminant. That number decides how many real solutions you get. If it's positive, you get two solutions; if it equals zero, you get just one repeated solution; and if it's negative, you get no real solutions at all. So always take a quick look at the discriminant first, it tells you what to expect. Now, if a quadratic does factorise nicely, there's a faster way using the Null Factor Law. The idea is simple: if a product equals zero, then at least one of the factors must be zero. So you find two numbers that multiply to the constant term and add to the middle coefficient, split the middle term, and factorise. Either method works, so you can pick whichever feels easier. Now let's see it in action.